{"causes":["The Scriptores Historiae Augustae were commissioned by Emperor Hadrian to create a comprehensive history of the Roman Empire.","The Roman Empire faced significant challenges during this period, including external threats from the Parthian Empire and the Germanic tribes.","The Augustan History was written during a time of relative stability and prosperity under the rule of the 'Five Good Emperors'."],"effects":["The Augustan History has been a primary source for historians studying the Roman Empire and its successor states.","The work has influenced the development of historical writing and has shaped the way we understand the Late Roman Empire.","The Augustan History has also had a lasting impact on the study of ancient Roman culture, politics, and society."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Scriptores Historiae Augustae","sourceName":"Scriptores Historiae Augustae","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"Augustan History","targetName":"Augustan History","context":"Commissioned by Emperor Hadrian to create a comprehensive history of the Roman Empire."},{"sourceSlug":"Roman Empire","sourceName":"Roman Empire","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"Augustan History","targetName":"Augustan History","context":"A primary source for historians studying the Roman Empire and its successor states."},{"sourceSlug":"Parthian Empire","sourceName":"Parthian Empire","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Augustan History","targetName":"Augustan History","context":"External threats from the Parthian Empire posed significant challenges to the Roman Empire during this period."},{"sourceSlug":"Five Good Emperors","sourceName":"Five Good Emperors","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Augustan History","targetName":"Augustan History","context":"Their reigns are depicted in vivid detail, providing a compelling counterpoint to the more turbulent episodes of imperial history."},{"sourceSlug":"Hellenistic kingdoms","sourceName":"Hellenistic kingdoms","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Augustan History","targetName":"Augustan History","context":"These interactions played a crucial role in shaping the Roman Empire's identity and its place within the ancient world."}],"places":[{"name":"Rome","role":"Imperial capital"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Augustan History","type":"Reference article","slug":"augustan-history"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q722678","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historia_Augusta"],"tags":["literary work","Histories & Chronicles","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Augustan History contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Historia Augusta (Latin: Scriptores Historiae Augustae, 'Writers of Augustan History') is a collection of biographies of Roman emperors from Hadrian (r. 117–138 CE) to Numerian and Carinus (d. 285 CE), purportedly written by six different authors in the reigns of Diocletian and Constantine (c. 295–330 CE), but almost certainly the work of a single forger writing c. 360–400 CE who invented most of the six 'authors', fabricated documents, letters, and quotations, and interpolated vast amounts of fictional material into what appears to be a continuation of Suetonius's Lives of the Twelve Caesars. The Historia Augusta is simultaneously the most controversial text in Latin historical literature — a documented forgery in which fiction is interwoven with genuine historical tradition in ways that remain impossible to disentangle — and an indispensable primary source for the history of the 2nd and 3rd century Roman Empire, particularly for the difficult period of the Crisis of the Third Century (235–284 CE) for which it is sometimes the only surviving narrative source.

The Historia Augusta's historical unreliability has been known since the 17th century, and modern scholarship (particularly the work of Hermann Dessau in 1889, who definitively demonstrated its 4th-century forgery character) has established that many of its supposed documents, quoted letters, and biographical details are invented. Yet for the period 235–284 CE — for which Cassius Dio, Herodian, and other historians either do not survive or break off — the Historia Augusta is sometimes the only narrative source, however unreliable. Its biographies of Gordian III, Philip the Arab, Decius, Valerian, and the 'soldier emperors' of the Crisis period are our primary (and deeply problematic) narrative accounts of one of the most turbulent periods in Roman imperial history.

The mystery of who wrote the Historia Augusta — what purpose the elaborate fiction of multiple authorship served, and why a 4th-century author would fabricate biographies of 2nd and 3rd century emperors — remains one of the great puzzles of ancient literary history, and the work continues to generate more scholarly controversy than almost any other Latin text.","subjects":["Roman History","Classical Era","Late Antiquity","Latin Literature","Historical Forgery","Roman Empire","Source Criticism","Biography"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Augustan History provides a captivating portrayal of the Late Roman Empire, a time of great transformation, cultural renewal, and imperial grandeur. As a window into the past, it continues to inspire scholars, historians, and enthusiasts alike, offering a glimpse into the world of ancient Rome's most fascinating and enigmatic rulers.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
